     <Vertical id="causes_list_full">
     <Markdown id="homeless_full_cause_list">
## Why Do People Experience Homelessness?

 - Trauma — domestic violence, family conflict, or untreated mental health conditions — can disrupt employment, relationships, and stability, leading to housing insecurity
 - Records such as an eviction filing or minor charges often appear in tenant screening databases, and many landlords automatically deny applications with that history
 - Limited economic opportunity, such as fewer jobs or restrictive labor-market conditions, can make it harder to find and keep stable employment
 - In some cities, there is not enough affordable housing for low- and middle-income residents
 - When people lose their jobs or have unstable employment and no savings, they may be unable to keep up with rent
 - Substance use challenges can make it more difficult to maintain steady employment or manage housing responsibilities
 - When wages remain low while rents continue to rise, even people working full time may struggle to afford housing
 - Racial discrimination in housing and lending markets can limit access to stable, affordable housing for some groups
 - Limited access to cash assistance or income-support programs can leave people without a financial safety net during crises
 - People who are unsheltered may face increased risks from extreme heat or severe weather, which can worsen health and housing instability
      </Markdown>
     </Vertical>
    </IntakeGate>

    <!-- Checklist of reasons to research -->
    <Vertical id="homeless_reasons_checklist">
